class.description:
[Horde2D] runs a crowd simulation in C++ and draws all its agents with one [MultiMesh] per [HordeType]. Agents seek a target ([member target] or [member target_position]), keep apart from each other, collide with static circular obstacles and stay inside optional rectangular [member bounds]. They have hit points and can be queried and damaged by area.
Agents are not nodes. Each one is identified by an integer id returned by [method spawn] and [method spawn_ring]. Ids stay valid until the agent dies; after that they are stale forever and never refer to a newer agent, so methods taking an id return [code]false[/code] or a zero value for them.
All positions are in this node's local coordinates. Keep the node at the origin with no rotation or scale if you want them to match global coordinates.
The simulation advances once per frame in [constant UPDATE_PROCESS] mode (default), once per physics tick in [constant UPDATE_PHYSICS] mode, or only when you call [method step] in [constant UPDATE_MANUAL] mode. Each update simulates, uploads the instance data, then emits [signal agents_died] and [signal target_contact] at most once each.
By default agents are drawn with a built-in shader that animates the [HordeType] sprite strip. Assign a [member CanvasItem.material] to this node to replace it: the shader receives [code]INSTANCE_CUSTOM.x[/code] (frame offset), [code].y[/code] (frames per second) and [code].z[/code] (frame count), and the type's [member HordeType.modulate] as the instance color.
[codeblock]
@onready var horde: Horde2D = $Horde2D

func _ready():
	var bat := HordeType.new()
	bat.radius = 5.0
	horde.target = $Player
	horde.spawn_ring(bat, Vector2.ZERO, 600.0, 2000)
	horde.agents_died.connect(_on_agents_died)
	horde.target_contact.connect(func(ids): $Player.hurt(ids.size()))

func _on_explosion(at: Vector2):
	horde.damage_circle(at, 64.0, 25.0)

func _on_agents_died(ids: PackedInt32Array, positions: PackedVector2Array):
	for p in positions:
		spawn_gem(p)
[/codeblock]
